2026-03-17 14:44:49 - DEBUG - all.stratus.diagnosis - [Loop 4] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='274f6711-1046-4281-a8f2-441460869fca'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='fd97df45-0128-4f24-9e17-9c2f1c54a68f'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='be90302f-5280-4975-9f87-4c539c823a09'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might be a root cause. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each one.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 411, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1903, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc38-b1cf-73c2-8b30-2893330a9d36-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 411, 'total_tokens': 1903}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5dd82137-5c40-4273-b882-0039a56f017a'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster, specifically in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The user mentioned using the get_services tool, which retrieves service names from Grafana. \n\nWait, but the tool description says it gets the list from Grafana. However, the application in question is the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is in the astronomy-shop namespace. Should I specify the namespace when using get_services? The tool's parameters don't mention a namespace argument. Hmm, maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to that namespace, or perhaps the tool doesn't require it. The user's initial instructions say to check the namespace, so maybe I need to confirm that.\n\nBut the get_services tool's parameters are empty, so it doesn't take any arguments. That means it might return all services in the cluster, not just the astronomy-shop namespace. That could be a problem. Wait, maybe the Grafana instance the tool is querying is already filtered to the correct namespace. Alternatively, maybe I need to use kubectl to list the services in the namespace first. But the user hasn't mentioned using kubectl yet. \n\nWait, the user's initial setup says the application belongs to the astronomy-shop namespace. So perhaps the get_services tool, when called, will return services specific to that namespace. But since the tool's parameters don't include a namespace, maybe it's designed to work within that context. Alternatively, maybe I need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the services in the namespace. For example, running 'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop'. \n\nBut the user's first step was to use get_services. Maybe the Grafana instance is already set up to show services in that namespace. However, if the tool doesn't take a namespace parameter, it might not be accurate. But since the user instructed to start with get_services, I should proceed with that. \n\nSo the tool call would be to get_services with no arguments. The response would give a list of services. Then, based on that list, I can proceed to check each service's health, metrics, dependencies, etc. \n\nAlternatively, if the get_services tool isn't scoped to the namespace, the list might include services from other namespaces, which would complicate things. But given the user's setup, perhaps the Grafana instance is already configured for the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nIn any case, the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. Then, once I have the services, I can move on to checking their metrics, traces, and dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98ae6dd5cd0cb1d3', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3052, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc38-c578-7f91-9d7d-f3d3c5ac7691-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98ae6dd5cd0cb1d3', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 579, 'total_tokens': 3052}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'email\', \'accounting\', \'currency\', \'cart\', \'quote\', \'ad\', \'product-catalog\', \'fraud-detection\', \'load-generator\', \'flagd\', \'payment\', \'image-provider\', \'checkout\', \'frontend\', \'shipping\', \'frontend-proxy\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='7f46c5d1-40d6-472c-89d6-ce04e53b6a06', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-98ae6dd5cd0cb1d3'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='cd22199d-862f-499c-a042-20fd78c2a23e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices application. They\'ve already used the get_services tool to list all the services in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace. The response gave a list of services like \'email\', \'accounting\', \'currency\', etc. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nSince the application is a microservices architecture, a problem in one service can affect others. The user needs to check the health and performance of each service. The available tools include get_traces, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, and others. \n\nFirst, maybe checking the dependency graph would show how services are interconnected. If there\'s a service that\'s not communicating properly with others, that could be a clue. The get_dependency_graph tool can show dependencies between services over a certain time frame. That might help identify if there\'s a service that\'s causing a ripple effect.\n\nAlternatively, using get_traces for each service could reveal if there are any errors or latency issues in their operations. But since there are many services, checking each one individually might be time-consuming. The dependency graph might be more efficient as it provides an overview of interactions.\n\nAnother approach is to look at metrics with get_metrics. For example, checking for high error rates or latency in specific services. But without knowing which service is problematic, it\'s hard to form the right PromQL query. \n\nThe user might also want to check the pods and deployments to see if any are not running as expected. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with \'kubectl get pods\' or \'kubectl get deployments\' in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace to check the status of workloads. If any pods are in a CrashLoopBackOff state or have errors, that could indicate a problem.\n\nBut since the user is supposed to identify faults and their root causes, starting with the dependency graph could help visualize which services are critical and where the bottlenecks or failures might be. If the graph shows a service with high latency or failed dependencies, that\'s a good starting point. \n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics for each service\'s health indicators (like request latency, error rates) could pinpoint specific issues. However, without knowing which service is faulty, it\'s a bit of a guess. \n\nConsidering the tools available, the get_dependency_graph might be the best first step after listing the services. It can provide a high-level view of service interactions, which can guide further investigation into specific services that might be causing or suffering from the issue.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \nThe `get_dependency_graph` tool will reveal how services interact and identify potential bottlenecks or failure points in the microservices architecture. By analyzing dependencies over the last 15 minutes, we can detect anomalies (e.g., missing calls, high latency, or failed service interactions) that may indicate root causes of faults. This provides a holistic view of the system\'s health and inter-service relationships.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_dependency_graph", "arguments": {"last_n_minutes": 15}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 642, 'prompt_tokens': 1719, 'total_tokens': 2361, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc38-e1a1-70d1-9769-df38c13d5871-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1719, 'output_tokens': 642, 'total_tokens': 2361}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='39b78085-be3e-4319-8c1f-f82641195acb'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me think. The user is trying to diagnose an application with many microservices. They started by getting the list of services using get_services, which returned a bunch of service names. Then they used get_dependency_graph to see how these services are connected. The next step is to check if there are any issues in the dependencies.\n\nWait, the user called get_dependency_graph with last_n_minutes set to 15. The response from the tool would be a JSON object representing the dependency graph. But the user hasn\'t shown the actual response yet. So, the next logical step is to analyze the dependency graph for any anomalies. However, since the user hasn\'t provided the tool\'s output, maybe they need to check specific services for traces or metrics.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the metrics for each service to see if there are any errors or high latencies. The get_metrics tool can be used with PromQL queries to check for errors or failed requests. For example, querying for error rates or latency metrics across the services. But first, they need to know which services are problematic. \n\nWait, the initial step was to get all services, then the dependency graph. Now, if the dependency graph shows some services not connecting properly, they can use get_traces for those specific services to look into the traces. Or maybe check the logs with exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. But the user hasn\'t seen the dependency graph results yet. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the status of deployments and pods to see if any are not running. Using kubectl commands like get deployments or get pods in the astronomy-shop namespace. But the available tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So, they could run \'kubectl get deployments\' or \'kubectl get pods\' to check the status of the workloads. \n\nBut the user\'s previous steps were focused on dependencies and traces. Maybe the next step is to check the metrics for each service. For example, using get_metrics with a query like "sum(rate(http_requests_total{status!~"5.."}[5m]))" to find services with high error rates. However, without knowing which services are faulty, it\'s hard to target the right query. \n\nAlternatively, the user might need to check the logs of specific services if there are known issues. But again, without prior information, it\'s a bit vague. The best approach might be to first check the dependency graph results to identify any services with unusual dependencies, then proceed to check their traces or metrics. \n\nWait, the user\'s last tool call was get_dependency_graph with 15 minutes. The response would be the dependency graph. If the graph shows that a service isn\'t being called or has high latency, then they can use get_traces for that service. But since the user hasn\'t provided the graph\'s output, the next step is to analyze the graph\'s data. However, in the current setup, the user can\'t see the graph\'s content. So, maybe the next step is to check the metrics for each service to find any anomalies. \n\nAlternatively, the user coul